Date: Wed, 17 Nov 1993 14:57:30 +0000 (GMT)
From: Charlie Stross <charless@sco.com>
Subject: Oceania vs. Cyberspatial Libertaria

szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo) writes:
>Subject: Oceania vs. Cyberspatial Libertaria
        :
>* A free independent jurisdiction is going to attract businesses 
>exluded by other jurisdictions.  These businesses will provide a large 
>source of funding for the basic real estate improvements, utilities, 
>etc. but they will also increase the threat of violent intervention 
>from outside.

>* Thus defense is paramount.  Lack of numbers and firepower must be
>more than made up for by mobility and stealth.

>* A basic form to consider is a "temporary autonomous zone" (TAZ).
        :

Hmm. Sorry: I'd like to believe in this, but current events in the UK
seem to suggest that governments can and will respond effectively to
this kind of threat.

A case in point is the rave culture; another case is the culture of 
New Age traveller convoys that troll around the English countryside.
The response the the UK government has been draconian and
illuminating.

The New Age convoys started in the early 80's, to the best of my
knowledge; unemployed/unemployable people going on the road in ancient
trucks and caravans (frequently army-surplus) and living off their
wits. In addition to social security -- which initially provided a
large chunk of their income -- they earned a living of sorts as
travelling performers and craftspeople. There was a hippy-ish element
to the subculture, and quite an interaction with the
rave/festival/pagan scenes.

Around the mid-80's the government began to get concerned about the
``threat to the fabric of society'' that these travellers represented.
This was code for the fact that, having no fixed abode, the travellers
could not easily be hunted down and forced to pay the poll tax.  A media
campaign accusing them of vandalism and damage to property was
orchestrated pretty thoroughly, and at the same time a fairly heavy
program of police harassment was instituted.  During the 80's the social
security regulations in the UK were tightened considerably, and
entitlement to benefits was withdrawn from several categories: including
people who didn't live at a fixed address for long enough to register.

There were some pretty notorious battles between riot police and
convoys, in which hundreds of police in riot gear, backed up by
cavalry and water cannon, destroyed vehicles, beat up and arrested
travellers, and forced them away from preferred camping sites. The
travellers were singled out for the vitriol of successive Home
Secretaries addressing Conservative Party conferences on the subject
of "Law and Order", and laws were passed rendering people who held
illegal festivals or raves liable for fairly severe prison sentences. 

There was also a proposal for legislation on camping that was so serious
that it was quietly dropped following an outcry by "decent, law-abiding"
owners of caravans and RV's who feared that they could be imprisoned for
the crime of going on a camping holiday.

The law as it applies to a travelling lifestyle in the UK is now so 
stringent that Romany (traditional gypsy) communities are pursuing
appeals through the European Court of Human Rights (contending that
they are the most oppressively anti-traveller laws seen in Europe
since the Second World War). These laws are also the subject of
considerable scrutiny by the National Council for Civil Liberties. The
government appears to be trying to prevent people from maintaining a
mobile lifestyle. 

Now I will not contend -- one way or the other -- that the new age
travellers are either a Good Thing or a Bad Thing. They're not _my_
thing (although my SO spent a year on the road during the 80's).
However they _are_ an example of a de facto TAZ, operating outside the
context of the law ... and the British government, which tends to be
relatively gentle with its citizens (compared to the US Federal
government) still managed a crack-down involving media villification,
riot police, legally implemented discrimination, and criminalization.
What's worse is that they managed to convince a large proportion of the
population that they were _right_, by portraying the travellers as an
anarchistic threat to the right of the British Public to Buy and Keep
real estate (a right that is as much a knee-jerk attribute of the
British cultural milieu as the right to keep and bear arms is in the US).

The point is, TAZ's are _temporary_. Because as soon as they are seen
to exist and to represent a threat, the state _WILL_ clamp down on
them. Not might. Not could. But Will.


-- Charlie
