Actually, I don't think that's the case at all. Tim May answered my question
with the kind of information that I expected to hear, namely, that P/E
ratios aren't the whole story in the "value" of a stock, therefore, a
truly wacky P/E isn't necessarily indicative of a market inefficiency.

In addition, Perry supplies an alternative hypothesis, that markets are
only long-run efficient, not necessarily short-run efficient. I'll
admit it's a plausible theory, but his tulip bulb example puts him in
agreement with J.K. Galbraith, a most unextropian fellow indeed. (Not
to say that Perry is necessarily wrong if he agrees with Galbraith,
only that it was a surprise for me.) I'm pretty sure I've got a
reference someplace for a person who will argue that even the famed
tulip bulb prices were rational at the time.

