Date: Wed, 17 Nov 93 10:58:07 PST
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: INVEST: Corn futures (was Re: Efficient Market Hypothesis...)

One of the several other Tim's (Freeman) writes:

> Is there any reason not to go into the corn storage business (or the
> heating oil storage business) and make a bundle, then?  This seems too
> easy.

Well, be careful in analyzing the "saving a bundle" comment Perry
made. 

Let's say that heating oil is cheaper in the summer than in the winter
(the exact details, or the commodity, aren't of central importance).
Does this mean that one should enter the heating oil storage business?

Some factors to consider:

- storage takes space: tanks, facilities, etc. This space costs money.

- storage can be danderous: fires, leaks, toxic waste, etc.

- oil prices _could_ fall, for a variety of reasons, even if the
_usual_ trend is for prices to rise in the winter.

- storing oil takes some experience in the biz...do Extropian-minded
folks really want to get into this business?

The physical storgage of oil can be bypassed by trading in oil futures
of course. However, the market in commodities is notoriously complex
and risky. And it's doubtful that a naive "outsider" would find
surefire profits.

(Good returns may be gotten by accepting higher risk, as in CAPM-style
portfolio analysis...as we have discussed in previous investment threads.)

Now all of these factors are why some people _do_ in fact store oil as
a business. But as a _business_, much like trucking or running a gas
station.

Speculators serve an important market function by taking on high risk
in exchange for high returns. The corn farmer is often more concerned
with getting a reasonable/stable price for his corn early on than he
is getting the highest prices later on if it means the risk that
prices will drop and he'll be wiped out.

The speculator can normally withstand these fluctuations. If not, he
shouldn't be putting his money in the commondities market.

> We may have different interpretations of what "efficient market"
> means.  In an efficient market, would the price of corn be constant,
> or would it accurately reflect nonzero storage and capital costs for
> holding the corn for a few months?  

No, the price would not be constant, for the reasons cited above.
See also material on the Black-Scholes model, the CAPM model, etc.

> (I'm not sure what you mean by "on contract".  If the co-op accepted
> delivery of the oil in the summer time and stored it until winter in a
> tank that would otherwise go idle, then it makes sense to me for them
> to make money from this because oil storage has value.  But I suspect
> you mean that they save money by buying a contract for winter-time oil
> delivery in the summer.  If they can save money by doing this, then
> maybe I should get into futures trading.)

Well, let us know how it turns out!  8-}

--Tim May

