Gee, there's a "barker/broker" who advertises on the Sonny Bloch show
about how you can do just this and make bundles ("but this investment
is speculative, and investors can lose all or part of their
investment" -- no kidding).

It's a little mindboggling to imagine that Perry's co-op in NYC has a
big enough oil tank to take summer delivery of next winter's oil,
unless the whole thing is an oil tank (then the fire insurance bill
would dwarf the oil savings :-). I think he meant they insulate
themselves from the winter price fluctuations by buying contracts in
summer. Then if oil goes up more than usual for some reason, they win.

I have relatives in NE Arkansas who each year sell their crops and
then plant them, laying off part of their risk against part of their
profit on the commodities market, and they have done quite well.

What's the exact definition of market efficiency again? It has to do
with market prices moving rapidly in response to new information about
future trends, so that at any given moment the price reflects an
informed consensus, right? Obviously the trading volume and turnover
in a market affect this, so we don't expect all markets to be equally
efficient (why, just look at HEx :-).

I think everyone who's in commodities and has enough information to be
there tends to get what he wants. Sometimes you want reduced risk and
sometimes you want ROI. Of course we know what usually happens to
fish who try to speculate in commodities without knowing what drives
the market.

