I trade in futures and derivatives, though mostly puts and calls on
stocks.

Suffice it to say that the beauty of such markets is that various
layers of a market can be peeled away and abstracted.

Thus, the person who think oil prices will rise need not physically
buy the oil and store it (I mentioned actual _storage_ of the oil or
corn in the context of the original question about becoming a grain
storer). Instead, one can, as Perry's coop does, buy a contract for
later delivery.

Even more abstraction is possible. One can simply buy contracts in
foobar on the bet/hunch they'll rise and then use the profits (if any,
of course) to buy the needed commodity when needed.

(The oil needed in February, for example. Thus, the issue of "making
money in the market" is completely abstracted away from any issues of
actually needing oil.)

