From: walter kehowski <wak@next11.math.pitt.edu>
Subject: Oceania vs. Cyberspatial Libertaria

####
Charlie Stroos says:

The New Age convoys started in the early 80's, to the best of my
knowledge; unemployed/unemployable people going on the road in ancient
trucks and caravans (frequently army-surplus) and living off their
wits. In addition to social security -- which initially provided a
large chunk of their income -- they earned a living of sorts as
travelling performers and craftspeople. There was a hippy-ish element
to the subculture, and quite an interaction with the
rave/festival/pagan scenes.

Around the mid-80's the government began to get concerned about the
``threat to the fabric of society'' that these travellers represented.
This was code for the fact that, having no fixed abode, the travellers
could not easily be hunted down and forced to pay the poll tax.  A media
campaign accusing them of vandalism and damage to property was
orchestrated pretty thoroughly, and at the same time a fairly heavy
program of police harassment was instituted.  During the 80's the social
security regulations in the UK were tightened considerably, and
entitlement to benefits was withdrawn from several categories: including
people who didn't live at a fixed address for long enough to register.
####

My reply:
The rave culture in England also were not too respectful of private property  
(littering, sewage, vandalism) and the citizenry complained. The proper function of  
govt. is to protect the citizen against such encroachments. I would not discount a  
tax evasion concern, however.

